 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "How did a horse named Hans solve math problems in Berlin?",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.041
  },
  {
   "text": "Two plus three? He taps his hoof five times, then stops. Perfect.",
   "s": 3.341,
   "e": 6.754
  },
  {
   "text": "His owner, a math teacher, swears he taught Hans himself.",
   "s": 6.954,
   "e": 9.636
  },
  {
   "text": "Crowds pack the courtyard, but scientists call it a cheap trick.",
   "s": 9.836,
   "e": 12.728
  },
  {
   "text": "So thirteen experts test him. A general, a vet, even a circus trainer.",
   "s": 12.928,
   "e": 16.986
  },
  {
   "text": "They ask fractions, dates, the time. Hans gets them right!",
   "s": 17.186,
   "e": 19.795
  },
  {
   "text": "Even with his owner gone, Hans keeps tapping the right answers!",
   "s": 19.995,
   "e": 22.55
  },
  {
   "text": "Newspapers call him the smartest horse alive. So how does he do it?",
   "s": 22.75,
   "e": 26.087
  },
  {
   "text": "Then a young scientist puts big blinders on Hans, and asks again.",
   "s": 26.387,
   "e": 29.551
  },
  {
   "text": "Hans taps, and taps, and taps, and never stops.",
   "s": 29.951,
   "e": 32.28
  },
  {
   "text": "Because Hans can't count at all. He watches faces.",
   "s": 32.48,
   "e": 35.002
  },
  {
   "text": "When he reaches the answer, the people watching relax, only a tiny bit, and he stops.",
   "s": 35.202,
   "e": 39.602
  },
  {
   "text": "He wasn't reading numbers. He was reading people.",
   "s": 39.802,
   "e": 42.184
  },
  {
   "text": "Which might be harder than math.",
   "s": 42.384,
   "e": 43.55
  }
 ],
